---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import CirculationSim from '../../simulators/circulation/CirculationSim.astro';
import { circulationQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/circulation';

const toc = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why animals need a circulatory system' },
  { id: 'parts', label: 'Hearts, vessels and blood' },
  { id: 'open-closed', label: 'Open and closed systems' },
  { id: 'single-double', label: 'Single and double circulation' },
  { id: 'examples', label: 'Comparing animals' },
  { id: 'activity', label: 'Circulation and activity' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world examples' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 40.1: Overview of the Circulatory System. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-1-overview-of-the-circulatory-system' },
  { text: 'Hill, R. W., Wyse, G. A., & Anderson, M. (2016). Animal Physiology (4th ed.). Sinauer Associates.' },
  { text: 'Schmidt-Nielsen, K. (1997). Animal Physiology: Adaptation and Environment (5th ed.). Cambridge University Press.' },
  { text: 'Hillyer, J. F., & Pass, G. (2020). The insect circulatory system: structure, function, and evolution. Annual Review of Entomology, 65, 121–143.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="circulatory-systems"
  description="Interactive animal circulatory systems simulator: compare the open circulation of an insect with the closed circulation of a fish, frog and mammal, follow a blood cell, and see how single and double circulation support activity."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={circulationQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A tiny flatworm can rely on substances simply diffusing through its body. A large, active animal cannot: diffusion is
      far too slow over more than a millimetre or so. Animals solve this with a circulatory system, but not all in the same
      way. Compare the very different hearts and vessels of an insect, a fish, a frog and a mammal, and follow the blood
      on its journey.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <CirculationSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose an <strong>animal</strong> and press <strong>Start</strong>. Blood flows around the schematic circuit; its colour shows how much oxygen it carries.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Follow one blood cell</strong> and read the note as the highlighted cell passes each heart chamber, artery, capillary bed and vein.</li>
      <li>Raise the <strong>activity level</strong>. Watch the heart rate and the oxygen in the returning blood change in the readouts.</li>
      <li>Use <strong>Inspect a blood vessel</strong> to compare an artery, a capillary and a vein, and compare the animals in the pressure chart.</li>
      <li>Click any organ, chamber or vessel for an explanation. <strong>Reset</strong> returns to the mammal at rest.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="why">Why animals need a circulatory system</h2>
  <p>
    Every cell needs a supply of oxygen and nutrients, and its carbon dioxide and other wastes must be removed. In very
    small or very flat animals, such as flatworms and jellyfish, these substances can diffuse directly between the cells
    and the surroundings. Diffusion is fast over tiny distances but extremely slow over larger ones. In bigger animals, a
    <strong>circulatory system</strong> moves a fluid around the body by bulk flow, carrying oxygen, nutrients, hormones,
    wastes, heat and immune cells, and keeping conditions around the cells stable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parts">Hearts, vessels and blood</h2>
  <p>
    A circulatory system has three parts: a <strong>pump</strong> (the heart), a <strong>fluid</strong> (blood or
    hemolymph) and, in most animals, <strong>vessels</strong>. In closed systems there are three kinds of vessel.
    <strong>Arteries</strong> carry blood away from the heart; their thick, muscular, elastic walls cope with high pressure.
    <strong>Capillaries</strong> have walls only one cell thick, so substances can diffuse between the blood and the cells.
    <strong>Veins</strong> return blood to the heart at low pressure and have valves to stop it flowing backwards.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open-closed">Open and closed systems</h2>
  <p>
    In an <strong>open circulatory system</strong>, found in insects and other arthropods and in most molluscs such as
    snails and clams, the heart pumps fluid called <strong>hemolymph</strong> into open spaces, the
    <strong>hemocoel</strong>, where it bathes the organs directly before returning to the heart through openings called
    ostia. Pressure is low and flow is slow. Insects get away with this because their oxygen arrives separately, through
    air tubes called <strong>tracheae</strong>. In a <strong>closed circulatory system</strong>, found in vertebrates,
    earthworms, and octopuses and squid, blood stays inside vessels. This allows higher pressure, faster flow, and
    control over how much blood each organ receives.
  </p>

  <h2 id="single-double">Single and double circulation</h2>
  <p>
    In <strong>single circulation</strong>, found in fish, blood passes through the heart once per circuit:
    heart → gills → body → heart. Blood loses much of its pressure in the narrow gill capillaries, so it reaches the body
    rather slowly. In <strong>double circulation</strong>, found in amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals, blood passes
    through the heart twice: once in the <strong>pulmonary circuit</strong> to the lungs and once in the
    <strong>systemic circuit</strong> to the body. After collecting oxygen, the blood returns to the heart to be pumped out
    again at high pressure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="examples">Comparing animals</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>Circulation</th><th>Heart</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Insect</strong></td><td>Open</td><td>Tube-like dorsal vessel</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fish</strong></td><td>Closed, single</td><td>2 chambers in a row</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Frog</strong></td><td>Closed, double</td><td>3 chambers</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mammal</strong></td><td>Closed, double</td><td>4 chambers</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    An insect’s oxygen is delivered by its tracheae, not its hemolymph. A fish heart holds only oxygen-poor blood. A
    frog’s two atria share one ventricle, so a little mixing happens, and its skin also exchanges gases. A mammal’s two
    circuits are completely separate. Most reptiles have a three-chambered heart with a partial wall in the ventricle; crocodiles, like birds and mammals,
    have four chambers. Birds and mammals evolved their four-chambered hearts independently.
  </p>

  <h2 id="activity">Circulation and activity</h2>
  <p>
    Animals with high metabolic rates, especially warm-blooded birds and mammals, need a lot of oxygen delivered quickly.
    During exercise the heart beats faster and pumps more blood, and active muscles extract more oxygen from it. In a
    resting human, blood returns to the heart still about 75% saturated with oxygen; during hard exercise it can return
    with less than a third. Fully separated, high-pressure circulation makes this possible.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Atrium / ventricle</dt>
    <dd>Atria receive blood returning to the heart; ventricles are the muscular chambers that pump it out.</dd>
    <dt>Hemolymph</dt>
    <dd>The circulating fluid of animals with open systems, such as insects.</dd>
    <dt>Hemocoel</dt>
    <dd>The body cavity through which hemolymph flows in an open system.</dd>
    <dt>Pulmonary and systemic circuits</dt>
    <dd>The routes from the heart to the lungs and back, and to the rest of the body and back.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world examples</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hummingbirds</strong> can have heart rates above 1,000 beats per minute while hovering.</li>
    <li><strong>Giraffes</strong> have unusually high blood pressure to pump blood up their long necks to the brain.</li>
    <li><strong>Octopuses</strong> have three hearts: two pump blood through the gills and one to the rest of the body.</li>
    <li><strong>Crabs and lobsters</strong> have blue hemolymph: its oxygen-carrying protein, hemocyanin, contains copper rather than iron.</li>
    <li><strong>Diving seals</strong> slow their hearts dramatically and send blood mainly to the brain and heart to save oxygen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“All animals have a heart like ours.”</strong> Hearts range from a simple tube to four chambers, and some animals have none at all.</li>
    <li><strong>“Oxygen-poor blood is blue.”</strong> It is dark red; blue is just a diagram colour code.</li>
    <li><strong>“Arteries always carry oxygen-rich blood.”</strong> Arteries carry blood away from the heart; the pulmonary artery and a fish’s ventral aorta carry oxygen-poor blood.</li>
    <li><strong>“An open system is badly designed.”</strong> It suits insects well, because their oxygen arrives by tracheae.</li>
    <li><strong>“A three-chambered heart mixes the blood completely.”</strong> Frog and reptile hearts keep the streams largely separate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The diagrams are schematic circuits, not anatomical drawings: real hearts and vessels branch to every organ, and
    blood flows in parallel through many capillary beds. Each animal stands for a large group: real species differ in
    heart rate, pressure and blood oxygen, so the values shown are approximate or illustrative. The frog’s skin
    circulation is merged with its lungs, and the mixing in its ventricle is simplified. Red and blue are a colour code
    for oxygen level. The insect’s hemolymph flow is a simple drift; real insects have extra pumps and partitions that
    steer it, and their heartbeat can even reverse direction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Circulatory systems carry oxygen, nutrients, hormones and wastes, and help keep internal conditions stable.</li>
    <li>Open systems (insects, most molluscs) bathe organs in hemolymph; closed systems keep blood in vessels.</li>
    <li>Fish have single circulation; amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals have double circulation.</li>
    <li>Hearts range from tubes to four chambers; complete separation supports high activity and warm-bloodedness.</li>
    <li>Arteries, capillaries and veins have different structures that suit their jobs.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
